{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Let Reuben live, and let him not die, and let his men be numbered.</b> This first blessing is of the same character as the blessings of Jacob, who said: ״יֶתֶר שְׂאֵת וְיֶתֶר עָז״ (Genesis 49:3). Behold, Jacob blessed him with two blessings, one called שְׂאֵת (exaltation) and one עֹז (might). For the שְׂאֵת is that his men would be numerous, so that they would require numbering, for a thing that is great in quantity requires a number, and that his men would be mighty; for Reuben, who dwelt on the frontier, needed all this — that his men be numerous and mighty — for then they would not die in war. Now, שְׂאֵת is an expression of numbering, as in ״כִּי תִשָּׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ״ (Exodus 30:11), ״נָשֹׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ״ (Numbers 4:22). And עָז is an expression of might. Now from the standpoint of his having disturbed his father's bed, he deserved that both be taken from him; but from the standpoint of blessing, he gave them to him as a gift and said: it is fitting that you should have ״יֶתֶר שְׂאֵת וְיֶתֶר עָז״ — more number and might, twofold over all the tribes — but ״פַּחַז כַּמַּיִם אַל תּוֹתַר״ (Genesis 49:4) — do not take the excesses; nevertheless, equal to all the tribes you shall be in שְׂאֵת and עֹז. And this is why Moses too blessed him with might, as it says: ״יְחִי רְאוּבֵן״ — that is to say, let him be a living man, mighty in battle, and then ״אַל יָמֹת״ (let him not die). ״וִיהִי מְתָיו מִסְפָּר״ — this is the matter of שְׂאֵת that we mentioned." "And he added ״וְאַל יָמֹת״ (and let him not die), because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tamid 32a): What should a person do and live? Let him kill himself. But he — let him live and not need to kill himself, as it is written (Psalms 89:49): ״מִי גֶבֶר יִחְיֶה וְלֹא יִרְאֶה מָוֶת״ — for if he keeps himself alive, then without doubt he kills himself, and so too the reverse. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 92a): ״יְחִי רְאוּבֵן״ — in this world; ״וְאַל יָמֹת״ — in the world to come. And since the world to come is concealed, therefore the life of the world to come remained in the precise wording of ״וְאַל יָמֹת״ by way of hint, and immediately adjacent to it is ״וְזֹאת לִיהוּדָה״, for he too was blessed in the world to come, because his bones rolled about, as Rashi explains. Or, because it says (Ezekiel 33:11): ״כִּי אִם בְּשׁוּב רָשָׁע מִדַּרְכּוֹ וְחָיָה״, and Reuben was a master of repentance — therefore it says: ״יְחִי רְאוּבֵן״ etc., followed immediately by ״וְזֹאת לִיהוּדָה״, for who caused Reuben to confess? Judah, etc. (Sotah 7b)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {1 "The phrase מָה יַעֲשֶׂה אָדָם וְיִחְיֶה יָמִית עַצְמוֹ is rendered literally as it stands, preserving the paradoxical Talmudic dictum (Tamid 32a) without paraphrase."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:32.302888810Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}